>
  <p>
    Most disappointment with
    <a href="/services/local-seo">SEO</a> and
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">ads</a> isn’t “Google is broken.” It’s mismatched expectations. This
    is the second piece we want owners to read before onboarding with
    <a href="/get-started">Local Service Rocket</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Marketing is a system, not a slot machine</h2>
  <p>
    Local Services Ads, Search ads, your Google Business Profile, and your website influence each
    other. Someone may click an ad, read reviews on the map pack, then call from the profile. Perfect
    source tags are often impossible, which is why we care more about
    <a href="/services/tracking">booked jobs and revenue vs spend</a> than a pretty pie chart.
  </p>

  <h2>Timelines that don’t lie to you</h2>
  <p>
    Ads can prove (or disprove) economics relatively fast when the site converts. Organic map-pack
    gains take longer and move with reviews, competition, and category relevance. A hot first month
    for a brand-new profile can fade in month two, that’s Google testing, not a permanent ceiling or
    a permanent victory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Raising budget 50% overnight can stall a working campaign while Google “learns.” Smaller steps,
    timed well, beat panic spend.
  </p>

  <h2>Plateaus are normal</h2>
  <p>
    Many shops can grow hard on ads and SEO into the low-mid hundreds of thousands per month, then
    need hiring, ops, and offer work to break the next ceiling. Marketing can’t invent trucks or
    closing skill. When lead flow is healthy and the calendar is full, the next bottleneck is usually
    <a href="/services/hiring">people</a>, not another agency pitch deck.
  </p>

  <h2>What you should expect from a partner</h2>
  <p>
    Clear stages, no fake guarantees, and honesty when the constraint is you (reviews, follow-up,
    pricing) versus us (campaigns, site, profile). If someone promises overnight domination with no
    work on your side, they’re selling a story, not a system.
  </p>
